  "grammar": [
    {
      "title": "Numbers with nouns",
      "body": "The number comes **before** the noun: மூணு புத்தகம் 'three books'. In speech the noun usually stays **singular** after a number (ரெண்டு காபி 'two coffees'); in writing the plural is common (மூன்று புத்தகங்கள்).\n\n'One' before a noun is **ஒரு** *oru*, which also works as 'a/an': ஒரு காபி 'a coffee'. Standing alone, 'one' is ஒண்ணு. Similarly, many numbers have short combining forms in compounds (இரு-, மு-, நால்-, ஐ-), e.g. இருநூறு 'two hundred', ஐநூறு 'five hundred'.\n\nFor people, 'all three of us' etc. use **-உம்**: நாங்க மூணு பேரும் 'all three of us'. பேர் *pēr* is the counter for people: எத்தனை பேர்? 'how many people?'.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "ஒரு டீ, ரெண்டு காபி.", "roman": "oru ṭī, reṇḍu kāpi.", "en": "One tea, two coffees." },
        { "native": "எங்க வீட்டுல அஞ்சு பேர்.", "roman": "enga vīṭṭula anju pēr.", "en": "There are five people in our house." },
        { "native": "எத்தன பேர் வர்றாங்க?", "roman": "ettana pēr varrānga?", "en": "How many people are coming?" },
        { "native": "நாங்க ரெண்டு பேரும் டீச்சர்.", "roman": "nānga reṇḍu pērum ṭīcchar.", "en": "We are both teachers." },
        { "native": "ஐநூறு ரூபா.", "roman": "ainūru rūbā.", "en": "Five hundred rupees." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Asking quantity and age",
      "body": "**எத்தனை** *ettanai* (spoken எத்தன) = 'how many' for things you count; **எவ்வளவு** *evvaḷavu* = 'how much' for amounts and prices.\n\nAge uses the dative: **எனக்கு இருபது வயசு** 'to me twenty age' = 'I am twenty'. Ask politely with **உங்களுக்கு என்ன வயசு?** or **உங்களுக்கு எத்தன வயசு?**. (Asking adults their age is not rude in India, though you may want to avoid it with women older than you.)",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "உங்க பையனுக்கு எத்தன வயசு?", "roman": "unga paiyanukku ettana vayasu?", "en": "How old is your son?" },
        { "native": "அவனுக்கு ஏழு வயசு.", "roman": "avanukku ēḻu vayasu.", "en": "He is seven." },
        { "native": "எத்தன கிலோ வேணும்?", "roman": "ettana kilō vēṇum?", "en": "How many kilos do you want?" },
        { "native": "எவ்வளவு தண்ணி வேணும்?", "roman": "evvaḷavu taṇṇi vēṇum?", "en": "How much water do you need?" }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Ordinals with -ஆவது, and fractions",
      "body": "Add **-ஆவது** *-āvadu* to a number to make an ordinal: மூணு → மூணாவது 'third', பத்து → பத்தாவது 'tenth'. The only exception is 'first', which is usually **முதல்** *mudal* (ஒண்ணாவது also exists for '1st standard' at school).\n\nFractions: **கால்** 'quarter', **அரை** 'half', **முக்கால்** 'three quarters'. Combine with -ஏ / -அரை: ரெண்டே கால் '2¼', ரெண்டரை '2½', ரெண்டே முக்கால் '2¾'. These are used constantly for time and weights.",
      "examples": [
        { "native": "முதல் நாள்", "roman": "mudal nāḷ", "en": "the first day" },
        { "native": "இது என் ரெண்டாவது வருஷம்.", "roman": "idu en reṇḍāvadu varusham.", "en": "This is my second year." },
        { "native": "அவ பத்தாவது படிக்கிறா.", "roman": "ava pattāvadu paḍikkirā.", "en": "She is in tenth standard." },
        { "native": "அரை கிலோ தக்காளி.", "roman": "arai kilō takkāḷi.", "en": "Half a kilo of tomatoes." },
        { "native": "ஒன்றரை லிட்டர்", "roman": "ondrarai liṭṭar", "en": "one and a half litres" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "dialogue": {
    "title": "At the fruit stall",
    "setting": "Sarah buys fruit from a street vendor (an older woman) in a Chennai market.",
    "lines": [
      { "speaker": "Vendor", "native": "வாங்கம்மா! என்ன வேணும்?", "roman": "vāngammā! enna vēṇum?", "en": "Come, madam! What would you like?" },
      { "speaker": "Sarah", "native": "வாழைப்பழம் ஒரு டஜன் எவ்வளவு?", "roman": "vāḻaippaḻam oru ḍajan evvaḷavu?", "en": "How much for a dozen bananas?" },
      { "speaker": "Vendor", "native": "அறுபது ரூபா.", "roman": "arubadu rūbā.", "en": "Sixty rupees." },
      { "speaker": "Sarah", "native": "சரி, ஒரு டஜன் குடுங்க. மாம்பழம் கிலோ எவ்வளவு?", "roman": "sari, oru ḍajan kuḍunga. māmbaḻam kilō evvaḷavu?", "en": "OK, give me a dozen. How much are mangoes per kilo?" },
      { "speaker": "Vendor", "native": "கிலோ நூத்தி இருபது.", "roman": "kilō nūtti irubadu.", "en": "A hundred and twenty a kilo." },
      { "speaker": "Sarah", "native": "அரை கிலோ மட்டும் போதும்.", "roman": "arai kilō maṭṭum pōdum.", "en": "Just half a kilo is enough." },
      { "speaker": "Vendor", "native": "சரிம்மா. இன்னும் ஏதாவது?", "roman": "sarimmā. innum ēdāvadu?", "en": "All right. Anything else?" },
      { "speaker": "Sarah", "native": "இல்ல, போதும். மொத்தம் எவ்வளவு ஆச்சு?", "roman": "illa, pōdum. mottam evvaḷavu ācchu?", "en": "No, that's enough. How much altogether?" },
      { "speaker": "Vendor", "native": "மொத்தம் நூத்தி இருபது ரூபா.", "roman": "mottam nūtti irubadu rūbā.", "en": "A hundred and twenty rupees in total." },
      { "speaker": "Sarah", "native": "இந்தாங்க. நன்றி!", "roman": "indānga. nandri!", "en": "Here you are. Thanks!" }
    ]
  },
  "culture": "In speech, 'hundred' in compound numbers becomes நூத்தி *nūtti* (written நூற்றி): 120 = நூத்தி இருபது. Indians count large sums in **lakhs** (லட்சம், 100,000) and **crores** (கோடி, 10 million), written 1,00,000 and 1,00,00,000. When handing over money or objects, use your right hand; இந்தாங்க *indānga* 'here, take it' is the polite word.",
  "lessons": [
